WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.838
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.286 --> 00:00:09.160
NISHA SHARMA: And for
the past several years,

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:11.980
I've been working on
our mobility solutions,

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:15.340
building all sorts of
applications, strategies,

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:17.950
helping our clients
build applications

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:21.370
for themselves and their
workers and employees.

00:00:21.370 --> 00:00:23.990
And today, I am here with
my colleague Phil Redman.

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:26.440
He'll be joining us
on stage shortly.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:29.140
And we're going to be talking
about the next generation

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:32.110
of mobile applications,
particularly powered

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:34.090
by Android and Google Cloud.

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:41.530
So with that, just
to kind of describe

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:45.820
a little bit about what we're
seeing, where we are, apps,

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:47.740
as we look at them--

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:49.040
I mean, they're not new.

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:51.920
We've been doing mobile
apps for a long time,

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:55.060
probably since the
late '90s really is

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:56.860
when we've been
building different types

00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:58.300
of mobile applications.

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:01.090
But over the years, we've seen
that the types of applications

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:06.090
we build and the interactions
and the integrations

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:08.380
that we have, all of these
things, are changing.

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:10.330
And with the next
generation of apps,

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:12.370
we're starting to see
new technologies that

00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:13.900
are being introduced
that allow us

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:17.650
to do even more powerful things
with Enterprise Mobility.

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:20.150
So we're seeing a focus more
on end-to-end solutions.

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:24.040
It's not just about the front
end or the back end or any one

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:24.550
component.

00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:27.370
But we have to look at it
from all of those things,

00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:29.590
plus cloud, plus security.

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:32.440
We really have to look at
these as end-to-end solutions.

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:35.230
We're seeing that a
lot of new innovation

00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:39.019
is being driven because of
the advances in technology.

00:01:39.019 --> 00:01:40.810
So for the past several
years, I would say,

00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:42.351
you know, honestly,
we haven't really

00:01:42.351 --> 00:01:45.160
been innovating in the
space of mobile apps.

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:48.220
We had been taking things
from desktop and paper

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:50.080
and then putting them
on mobile devices.

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:52.960
But we hadn't really
been doing new things

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:54.970
with those mobile applications.

00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:57.740
And so we'll talk a
lot more about that.

00:01:57.740 --> 00:01:59.270
And then in order
to support that,

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:01.600
there are a lot of
things that need

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:04.090
to happen on the back
end side to support

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:05.390
those new applications.

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:08.919
And in many, many cases, we
don't have access to data today

00:02:08.919 --> 00:02:10.449
that we need to have access to.

00:02:10.449 --> 00:02:13.210
Or perhaps the back
end systems just

00:02:13.210 --> 00:02:15.480
aren't able to support
what we need to do.

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:18.430
And so we have a number of new
types of back end architectures

00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:20.470
and platform capabilities
that really enable

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:24.320
us to do things in that space.

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:26.789
So here's the agenda real quick.

00:02:26.789 --> 00:02:29.330
We're going to talk about the
evolution of mobile apps, where

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:32.360
we were, where we're going,
look into the next generation

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:34.910
of mobile apps, and then
talk about how we're really

00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:37.010
transforming those
apps in the enterprise,

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:39.170
and then some of our
recommendations for how

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:43.050
to build next
generation applications.

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:46.220
So if we look at
the overall timeline

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:50.510
of mobile applications, like I
said, this chart goes to 2005.

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:52.430
But in reality,
like I said, we've

00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:54.380
been doing this
since the late '90s.

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:56.030
I mean, you might remember--

00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:58.190
maybe some of you
might remember--

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:01.580
BlackBerrys and Windows mobile
devices and Windows CE devices

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:03.690
and those types of applications.

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:07.610
And so we look at
that time as what we

00:03:07.610 --> 00:03:10.160
call Enterprise Mobility 1.0.

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:12.140
And it was all
about more just, how

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:13.550
do I get email on my devices?

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:14.960
How do I get calendar?

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:16.730
How do I do text messaging?

00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:19.550
Very simple types
of applications

00:03:19.550 --> 00:03:20.960
that we were doing.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:22.940
And then we started
to shift more

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:26.000
towards Enterprise
Mobility 2.0, which

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:27.470
is where we really
started to take

00:03:27.470 --> 00:03:29.630
a more mobile first approach.

00:03:29.630 --> 00:03:32.870
So like I said, it wasn't about
taking desktop applications

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:34.550
and putting them on mobile.

00:03:34.550 --> 00:03:36.470
But now we're really
looking at, what

00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:40.610
are those use cases that
really apply to mobile devices

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:42.680
to allow us to take
advantage of what's happening

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:43.680
on those mobile devices?

00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:49.820
And then we started to move
towards Enterprise Mobility

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:52.210
3.0, which is
where we are today.

00:03:52.210 --> 00:03:54.050
But we're shifting into
Enterprise Mobility

00:03:54.050 --> 00:03:57.070
4.0 as well, or the
next generation really.

00:03:57.070 --> 00:04:01.190
And Enterprise Mobility 3.0
is more about transforming

00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:03.080
how we're doing work today.

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:05.660
It's about introducing
new types of technologies

00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:09.410
that, like I said, it's
not about now going

00:04:09.410 --> 00:04:11.330
from paper to the
phone, but it's

00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:14.660
about how do we
really change the way

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:16.730
our workers do their jobs.

00:04:16.730 --> 00:04:18.350
And so we'll show
you some examples

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:19.820
of how we're doing that.

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:25.330
Oh, sorry, but I'll
also say is you

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:28.020
can see here that the
types of applications

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:30.940
that we were building have
evolved over time as well.

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:34.020
So we've had mobile websites,
native apps, web apps,

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:34.740
hybrid apps.

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:35.940
All of this has changed.

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:38.830
We still continue to use
these types of applications.

00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:42.150
But we continue to see newer
and newer types of applications

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:42.930
evolve.

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:45.870
And then the way we
interact with the data,

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:48.450
with our devices,
that's also changing.

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:51.520
So it used to be way
back when we had a stylus

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:55.050
and we had to tap on
our device and our app.

00:04:55.050 --> 00:04:58.050
Then we started to be
able to use our finger.

00:04:58.050 --> 00:05:02.940
Then we introduced gestures, so
the device could see your eyes

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:05.340
moving on the screen.

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:06.960
And then taking
advantage of some

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:08.730
of the context
aware capabilities,

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:11.880
so that the device
knew where we were.

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:14.910
It knew a little bit
more about the situation,

00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:17.430
the present situation, and
perhaps even the past situation

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:21.630
to provide new ways
to interact with us.

00:05:21.630 --> 00:05:23.660
We're doing a lot
more with voice now.

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:27.990
So with the emergence of
voice assistance and voice

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:31.940
capabilities, we're seeing a
lot of voice to text and text

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:35.100
to voice to allow us to interact
a little bit differently.

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:37.030
And then, of course,
artificial intelligence,

00:05:37.030 --> 00:05:40.720
which we'll talk about a
little bit here as well.

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:42.810
So while there's all
these advancements

00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:45.120
in these technologies,
enterprises are actually

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:47.580
quite slow to adopt these.

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:49.230
And if you think
about ourselves,

00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:54.090
in our own everyday
use of our own devices,

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:56.430
the average consumer has
about 30 applications

00:05:56.430 --> 00:05:58.170
on their phones.

00:05:58.170 --> 00:06:00.660
But if you look at
the enterprise side,

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:03.740
the average is about eight.

00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:05.940
But it's growing,
and it's changing.

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:10.290
And we're going to take a look
at some of the challenges that

00:06:10.290 --> 00:06:11.140
are behind that.

00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:14.940
But the types of applications
that enterprises do

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:19.590
use and do buy, we consider
off-the-shelf or packaged

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:21.840
applications or
packaged applications

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:24.630
that might require some
level of customization

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:28.530
or some level of personalization
to be able to use them.

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:31.650
And then on the other side, we
have total custom applications.

00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:34.707
So these are apps that have
been built from scratch.

00:06:34.707 --> 00:06:36.540
And so you've designed
the whole experience.

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:38.910
You've built everything,
perhaps using

00:06:38.910 --> 00:06:41.220
design-led thinking
or new approaches

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:43.060
to designing applications.

00:06:43.060 --> 00:06:44.520
But they are very custom.

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:49.530
But when you put that together,
you can see that about 70%

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:51.300
of the applications
that we're building

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:53.880
have some sort of
development required

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:55.950
in order to actually use them.

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:00.630
But going to some
of the challenges

00:07:00.630 --> 00:07:03.900
of why it's difficult, we will
talk about some of these here.

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:08.130
So if you look at the
platform challenges,

00:07:08.130 --> 00:07:09.870
even if you just look
at Android itself,

00:07:09.870 --> 00:07:11.690
as I'm sure you
all know, Android

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:13.620
has got many different versions.

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:16.710
And different features
work on different versions.

00:07:16.710 --> 00:07:18.430
And so we have to
account for those.

00:07:18.430 --> 00:07:20.430
And then you start to
introduce other platforms.

00:07:20.430 --> 00:07:24.786
Maybe you're also supporting iOS
or maybe you've got mobile web.

00:07:24.786 --> 00:07:26.160
And then you really
need to start

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:27.952
to understand, what
can I do on each

00:07:27.952 --> 00:07:29.160
of those different platforms?

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:30.530
How do I manage that?

00:07:30.530 --> 00:07:32.220
And how do I account for that?

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:34.860
Ultimately, then means that you
have higher development costs

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:37.020
and operating costs,
because as you

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:39.900
may know, for each of the
different types of applications

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:42.840
you have to build, that just
requires more development

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:44.880
work to make it happen.

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:48.570
Enterprises usually have
limited resources and knowledge.

00:07:48.570 --> 00:07:52.252
They don't necessarily have
the expertise in security.

00:07:52.252 --> 00:07:54.210
Or perhaps they don't
have the right designers.

00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:56.580
Or perhaps they don't
have the experts

00:07:56.580 --> 00:07:58.717
in the back end
integration or security

00:07:58.717 --> 00:08:00.300
or even the right
front end developers

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:03.000
to be able to build some
of the applications.

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:05.700
And sometimes business
cases are hard to prove out

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:07.230
for many companies.

00:08:07.230 --> 00:08:10.260
And they can't always
demonstrate the value

00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:12.400
that they're getting from
the mobile applications.

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:14.610
And then when you compare
that to the cost, sometimes

00:08:14.610 --> 00:08:17.610
the cost just outweighs
the perceived value.

00:08:17.610 --> 00:08:21.150
And the last one there, the
constant kind of tension

00:08:21.150 --> 00:08:23.520
between business and IT.

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:26.820
Oftentimes businesses look
to IT and the IT groups

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:30.780
can't support them as quickly
as the businesses might want.

00:08:30.780 --> 00:08:33.390
Or perhaps the IT groups are
looking at the businesses

00:08:33.390 --> 00:08:36.630
and saying, what you're trying
to do is really not reasonable,

00:08:36.630 --> 00:08:38.400
or it's just not
possible, or maybe it's

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:39.816
going to take a
little bit of time

00:08:39.816 --> 00:08:42.240
because we have to put some
things in place in order

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:43.679
to support that.

00:08:43.679 --> 00:08:46.540
So there is this
kind of challenge.

00:08:46.540 --> 00:08:51.010
But let's talk about the next
generation of applications.

00:08:51.010 --> 00:08:55.470
And as I mentioned, this journey
from Moblilty 1.0 to 3.0--

00:08:55.470 --> 00:08:59.240
and I think Google Maps
is a great example of that

00:08:59.240 --> 00:09:01.140
and something we can
all relate to right.

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:05.430
So if we think back to the
early days of Google Maps,

00:09:05.430 --> 00:09:07.870
really all you could
do was get directions.

00:09:07.870 --> 00:09:09.500
You had to put in
an address, and it

00:09:09.500 --> 00:09:10.970
would give you directions.

00:09:10.970 --> 00:09:12.540
And at one point,
you actually had

00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:14.600
to print out a list
of the directions,

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:17.770
because it wasn't actually
navigating you there.

00:09:17.770 --> 00:09:19.770
So then in Mobility
2.0, we started

00:09:19.770 --> 00:09:24.300
to see things like navigation
and traffic introduced.

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:26.460
And we could plug
in multiple points.

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:29.910
So it could actually suggest
the right routes and ways

00:09:29.910 --> 00:09:31.790
to go right and
things like that.

00:09:31.790 --> 00:09:33.900
So we started to get a
little bit more advanced.

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:36.150
And with Mobility
3.0, now we're really

00:09:36.150 --> 00:09:38.190
using maps in a
very different way.

00:09:38.190 --> 00:09:41.070
It's not just about getting
directions or figuring out

00:09:41.070 --> 00:09:42.360
how to get somewhere.

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:45.180
But it's about exploring places.

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:51.240
Now when you look up something,
you go to Maps and you search,

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:52.350
you have reviews.

00:09:52.350 --> 00:09:55.380
You've got other
location information.

00:09:55.380 --> 00:09:58.560
Perhaps you've got
hours of business.

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:00.930
There's just a lot
more information there.

00:10:00.930 --> 00:10:02.090
You're able to share.

00:10:02.090 --> 00:10:04.270
Now Maps is integrated
with more things.

00:10:04.270 --> 00:10:07.130
So even Google Assistant
is integrated with Maps.

00:10:07.130 --> 00:10:09.500
So it knows what
your habits are.

00:10:09.500 --> 00:10:12.006
It knows which location
is home or work.

00:10:12.006 --> 00:10:13.380
And it can suggest,
hey, you have

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:14.546
an appointment at this time.

00:10:14.546 --> 00:10:17.700
You should leave at this time
in order to get there in time.

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:21.090
So it's just really changed
the way we're using Maps.

00:10:21.090 --> 00:10:23.170
It's not just about
directions anymore.

00:10:23.170 --> 00:10:26.460
So hopefully you can see how
over time, the applications

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:29.100
and technology have
allowed us to build

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:32.160
more exciting applications.

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:34.510
So on the consumer side--

00:10:34.510 --> 00:10:37.950
that's a consumer application--
but the enterprise expectations

00:10:37.950 --> 00:10:39.862
are continuing to
increase because

00:10:39.862 --> 00:10:41.820
of these really cool
consumer applications that

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:43.060
are out there.

00:10:43.060 --> 00:10:45.900
So some of these, for example,
mobile business models,

00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:49.950
I mean the Uber models or
the micro-payments or mobile

00:10:49.950 --> 00:10:52.320
advertising, these
are all capabilities

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:55.080
that businesses can take
advantage of and include

00:10:55.080 --> 00:10:57.280
in their enterprise
solutions as well.

00:10:57.280 --> 00:11:00.290
We expect these really
personalized experience.

00:11:00.290 --> 00:11:02.130
Just like how we get
in the consumer apps,

00:11:02.130 --> 00:11:04.890
we want those things for
our work applications too.

00:11:04.890 --> 00:11:08.310
We want to be able to use social
media types of applications

00:11:08.310 --> 00:11:11.790
and be able to share and
collaborate with other people

00:11:11.790 --> 00:11:12.960
that we work with.

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:14.730
Contextual awareness--
our application

00:11:14.730 --> 00:11:18.090
should know that perhaps I
did a particular function

00:11:18.090 --> 00:11:20.160
on my desktop and
now I'm picking it up

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:24.330
on my phone or my tablet
or on a different device,

00:11:24.330 --> 00:11:28.290
and taking all that
information into account.

00:11:28.290 --> 00:11:31.230
The payment capabilities-- and
then the natural and intuitive

00:11:31.230 --> 00:11:34.080
interfaces, this is something
I think enterprises typically

00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:35.780
didn't really focus
on this in the past.

00:11:35.780 --> 00:11:38.791
It was really just, you know,
hey, it's for our employees.

00:11:38.791 --> 00:11:40.290
They just need
something that works.

00:11:40.290 --> 00:11:43.390
And they don't really focus
on that user experience.

00:11:43.390 --> 00:11:45.390
And then they found that
their employees weren't

00:11:45.390 --> 00:11:47.520
happy with the applications.

00:11:47.520 --> 00:11:49.170
They were complaining about it.

00:11:49.170 --> 00:11:52.350
I was recently at a
pretty major store,

00:11:52.350 --> 00:11:55.020
and they had rolled out
a new mobile application

00:11:55.020 --> 00:11:59.400
to all the cashiers
and store workers.

00:11:59.400 --> 00:12:01.890
And all the people,
like all their employees

00:12:01.890 --> 00:12:05.050
were complaining about this
app to me as a customer.

00:12:05.050 --> 00:12:06.510
They're like, I
can't use this app.

00:12:06.510 --> 00:12:07.050
It's terrible.

00:12:07.050 --> 00:12:09.258
We can't do like what we
used to do on the other one.

00:12:09.258 --> 00:12:10.889
Or you know, I can't
figure out like--

00:12:10.889 --> 00:12:12.180
and they were just complaining.

00:12:12.180 --> 00:12:14.670
And that just really
shows the importance

00:12:14.670 --> 00:12:19.290
of having these really
good interfaces and designs

00:12:19.290 --> 00:12:22.750
with our enterprise
applications as well.

00:12:22.750 --> 00:12:25.170
So the nice thing is that
we've got these really great

00:12:25.170 --> 00:12:27.720
technologies now that are
allowing us to provide

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:29.250
these better experiences.

00:12:29.250 --> 00:12:33.180
So voice assistance, we've
got Google Assistant.

00:12:33.180 --> 00:12:34.350
We've got Google Home.

00:12:34.350 --> 00:12:37.470
We've got Dialogflow
to allow us to create

00:12:37.470 --> 00:12:41.760
conversations and provide
a more conversational type

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:43.050
of experience.

00:12:43.050 --> 00:12:44.760
Immersive applications
are augmented

00:12:44.760 --> 00:12:46.350
reality and virtual reality.

00:12:46.350 --> 00:12:50.850
So we're able to use ARCore and
Daydream and the SDKs and all

00:12:50.850 --> 00:12:52.830
that they provide, so
that we can, again,

00:12:52.830 --> 00:12:57.170
provide these really exciting
experiences through AR and BR.

00:12:57.170 --> 00:12:59.160
IoT applications,
of course, being

00:12:59.160 --> 00:13:03.960
able to use sensors all around
us that we can communicate

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:06.750
with and provide
additional information

00:13:06.750 --> 00:13:10.470
or additional capabilities
that we didn't have before.

00:13:10.470 --> 00:13:12.139
We mentioned the
context of where

00:13:12.139 --> 00:13:13.680
machine learning
and AI-- and I think

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:15.550
this is one of the
most exciting places.

00:13:15.550 --> 00:13:18.570
And [? Faitbeh ?] was
talking about that as well.

00:13:18.570 --> 00:13:20.520
But we're now building
in capabilities

00:13:20.520 --> 00:13:24.630
such as natural language
processing, image recognition,

00:13:24.630 --> 00:13:27.180
prediction, all of
these capabilities

00:13:27.180 --> 00:13:31.680
to allow us to make
our apps smarter.

00:13:31.680 --> 00:13:34.470
And then Instant Apps, which
Phil will show is a demo about,

00:13:34.470 --> 00:13:36.780
but it's really being
able to take action

00:13:36.780 --> 00:13:41.250
at that point of desire, rather
than having to go and download

00:13:41.250 --> 00:13:43.830
applications and find out
where in the application

00:13:43.830 --> 00:13:44.730
you need to go.

00:13:44.730 --> 00:13:46.230
But these are just
instant functions

00:13:46.230 --> 00:13:49.070
that we can do right away.

00:13:49.070 --> 00:13:52.180
So a lot of advancements
on the device itself.

00:13:52.180 --> 00:13:54.880
If you think about the
smartphones that you all have,

00:13:54.880 --> 00:13:57.190
just look year after
year at all the changes

00:13:57.190 --> 00:14:01.360
that you see in terms of device
processing time, where now

00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:04.690
we've got multicore processors
that can be 2 and 1/2 times

00:14:04.690 --> 00:14:07.000
faster than single
core processors.

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:09.640
That allows us to
do the AR and the VR

00:14:09.640 --> 00:14:12.880
and the machine learning
on the devices, especially

00:14:12.880 --> 00:14:17.230
the Edge analytics type of
work that we can do as well.

00:14:17.230 --> 00:14:19.060
The screens and the
cameras, every year

00:14:19.060 --> 00:14:20.920
there's this kind of
competition to see

00:14:20.920 --> 00:14:23.500
who has the better camera.

00:14:23.500 --> 00:14:26.800
And it's always about us
taking selfies and pictures.

00:14:26.800 --> 00:14:30.250
But the cameras and
the screen resolution

00:14:30.250 --> 00:14:33.520
really allow us to get much
more accurate and precise

00:14:33.520 --> 00:14:36.700
with things like image
recognition or AR/VR.

00:14:36.700 --> 00:14:42.550
And so it allows us to do a
lot more with that information.

00:14:42.550 --> 00:14:46.330
The sensors, we talked about
as well-- fingerprint sensors,

00:14:46.330 --> 00:14:51.310
accelerometers, ambient
light sensors, and so on.

00:14:51.310 --> 00:14:54.070
And then wearables
as well, we have

00:14:54.070 --> 00:14:56.770
a wide variety of
whether it's watches

00:14:56.770 --> 00:15:00.490
or glasses or any other
number of wearable devices

00:15:00.490 --> 00:15:03.920
that allow us to have a
much better experience.

00:15:03.920 --> 00:15:05.496
And so as you look
at all of these,

00:15:05.496 --> 00:15:07.120
the nice thing with
Android is that you

00:15:07.120 --> 00:15:11.710
have a variety of devices and
form factors and price points.

00:15:11.710 --> 00:15:13.900
So enterprises can
pick the right device

00:15:13.900 --> 00:15:15.907
that meets their
needs and matches

00:15:15.907 --> 00:15:16.990
what they're trying to do.

00:15:20.050 --> 00:15:25.000
So some examples of some of the
technologies that are providing

00:15:25.000 --> 00:15:26.530
these experiences.

00:15:26.530 --> 00:15:28.200
We've already talked
a bit about context

00:15:28.200 --> 00:15:31.810
aware and how our applications
can adapt to our environment

00:15:31.810 --> 00:15:35.680
or to our situation or
to whatever it might be.

00:15:35.680 --> 00:15:39.280
The AI powered applications,
again our applications

00:15:39.280 --> 00:15:40.180
are getting smarter.

00:15:40.180 --> 00:15:43.480
They're learning more
about all sorts of things.

00:15:43.480 --> 00:15:45.790
They're taking advantage
of a lot of data

00:15:45.790 --> 00:15:48.670
that we hadn't had
before, getting

00:15:48.670 --> 00:15:53.140
into recommendations, into
personalization, and so on.

00:15:53.140 --> 00:15:54.640
Self-learning
applications-- so this

00:15:54.640 --> 00:15:56.890
is about the app
learning itself and how

00:15:56.890 --> 00:16:01.360
it can take different actions
based on what it's experiencing

00:16:01.360 --> 00:16:03.810
and what it's been
learning over time.

00:16:03.810 --> 00:16:06.500
And Edge analytics,
this is the ability to--

00:16:06.500 --> 00:16:09.340
you may be familiar with
the TensorFlow and Cloud

00:16:09.340 --> 00:16:12.820
ML and these kinds of image
recognition capabilities

00:16:12.820 --> 00:16:15.280
that we've been doing
for a few years.

00:16:15.280 --> 00:16:16.520
But that's in the cloud.

00:16:16.520 --> 00:16:18.160
And now we can actually
do these things

00:16:18.160 --> 00:16:21.310
with TensorFlow Lite and
some of the neural networks

00:16:21.310 --> 00:16:22.766
API and things like that.

00:16:22.766 --> 00:16:24.140
We can do it right
on the device.

00:16:24.140 --> 00:16:25.723
We don't even need
to go to the cloud.

00:16:25.723 --> 00:16:28.640
And so that allows us to
do things in more real time

00:16:28.640 --> 00:16:31.840
and just get a much better
experience in that way, which

00:16:31.840 --> 00:16:34.570
is really exciting.

00:16:34.570 --> 00:16:38.890
IoT-based applications,
we're seeing

00:16:38.890 --> 00:16:40.600
lots of examples of those.

00:16:40.600 --> 00:16:42.110
You can see those
in health care.

00:16:42.110 --> 00:16:43.390
You can see those in retail.

00:16:43.390 --> 00:16:45.190
You can see those
in manufacturing

00:16:45.190 --> 00:16:46.690
and so on, where
we're really trying

00:16:46.690 --> 00:16:50.380
to take advantage of the
sensors and the other machines

00:16:50.380 --> 00:16:54.310
that are around us to try to
provide a better experience.

00:16:54.310 --> 00:16:58.240
Immersive applications,
voice assisted, templatize--

00:16:58.240 --> 00:17:00.190
templatize applications,
we list this here,

00:17:00.190 --> 00:17:03.550
but I haven't really seen a
lot of success with those.

00:17:03.550 --> 00:17:05.680
That's really where
third-party providers

00:17:05.680 --> 00:17:08.680
might provide a template
type of an application, which

00:17:08.680 --> 00:17:13.030
you would build off of
and perhaps customize

00:17:13.030 --> 00:17:15.520
a lot more than normal.

00:17:15.520 --> 00:17:18.069
But many have tried
it, but it's just not

00:17:18.069 --> 00:17:23.550
as successful from some of
what I've personally seen.

00:17:23.550 --> 00:17:26.369
So now, I'm going to
transition it over to Phil.

00:17:26.369 --> 00:17:29.550
And Phil will come and
first do our first demo

00:17:29.550 --> 00:17:31.460
with our voice-based
digital system.

00:17:31.460 --> 00:17:31.960
Phil.

00:17:35.640 --> 00:17:36.711
PHILLIP REDMAN: Great.

00:17:36.711 --> 00:17:37.210
Great.

00:17:37.210 --> 00:17:38.173
Can you hear me OK?

00:17:38.173 --> 00:17:39.604
Thank you, Nisha.

00:17:39.604 --> 00:17:40.270
I'm Phil Redman.

00:17:40.270 --> 00:17:43.140
I'm in a part of our
digital transformation team.

00:17:43.140 --> 00:17:45.970
So I work with clients
globally to help them

00:17:45.970 --> 00:17:50.650
around mobility, security, big
data and analytics, AI machine

00:17:50.650 --> 00:17:53.296
learning, and of course
mobile technologies.

00:17:53.296 --> 00:17:54.670
And so we're
working with clients

00:17:54.670 --> 00:17:56.807
to help transform
what they're doing.

00:17:56.807 --> 00:17:58.390
And here's an example
of one that I'll

00:17:58.390 --> 00:18:00.280
show you is about an
organization called

00:18:00.280 --> 00:18:01.930
West Midlands Police.

00:18:01.930 --> 00:18:04.420
They're the second largest
police force in the UK,

00:18:04.420 --> 00:18:07.000
so over 10,000
police officers, just

00:18:07.000 --> 00:18:09.940
a bit smaller than the London
police force, the London Metro.

00:18:09.940 --> 00:18:11.920
And they're really
looking at how

00:18:11.920 --> 00:18:15.880
to modernize the police
force going forward and using

00:18:15.880 --> 00:18:18.417
mobile devices, specifically
Android mobile devices.

00:18:18.417 --> 00:18:20.500
And so we worked with them
to get an understanding

00:18:20.500 --> 00:18:24.530
of how we can accommodate
police officers as they're

00:18:24.530 --> 00:18:27.010
going towards
different incidences

00:18:27.010 --> 00:18:29.515
and how to support them when
they get to those instances.

00:18:29.515 --> 00:18:31.390
So we're going to show
you an example of what

00:18:31.390 --> 00:18:32.422
that looks like.

00:18:32.422 --> 00:18:33.880
And we've created
what we call part

00:18:33.880 --> 00:18:36.087
of a digital virtual
mobile system.

00:18:36.087 --> 00:18:38.170
Now you might be familiar
with virtual assistants.

00:18:38.170 --> 00:18:39.950
And there's lots
of varieties here.

00:18:39.950 --> 00:18:42.720
But it's an idea of more of
a voice-based interaction

00:18:42.720 --> 00:18:43.720
for the police officers.

00:18:43.720 --> 00:18:48.190
Obviously, in many cases, police
officers will be in their cars.

00:18:48.190 --> 00:18:49.630
They may be by themselves.

00:18:49.630 --> 00:18:53.055
They may not have hands to be
able to navigate information.

00:18:53.055 --> 00:18:55.180
At the same time, they want
to find out information

00:18:55.180 --> 00:18:55.870
when they're on the move.

00:18:55.870 --> 00:18:57.494
They don't have a
lot of time once they

00:18:57.494 --> 00:18:59.680
get to what they call
an incident to find out

00:18:59.680 --> 00:19:00.954
that information.

00:19:00.954 --> 00:19:02.620
So we worked together
with them in order

00:19:02.620 --> 00:19:06.550
to understand how they can
better be better supported

00:19:06.550 --> 00:19:08.290
by having information
at their hands

00:19:08.290 --> 00:19:11.140
and understanding what's going
on as they arrive, and then

00:19:11.140 --> 00:19:14.140
improves on the process that
they have once they arrive too,

00:19:14.140 --> 00:19:16.324
all by using a digital
virtual assistant.

00:19:16.324 --> 00:19:18.490
So just like we may use a
digital virtual assistant,

00:19:18.490 --> 00:19:21.670
like Google Assistant
or Siri in order

00:19:21.670 --> 00:19:24.010
to help us with our task--
we're taking messages

00:19:24.010 --> 00:19:25.732
or making phone calls
or doing searches--

00:19:25.732 --> 00:19:27.190
they're looking to
see how they can

00:19:27.190 --> 00:19:29.920
use voice-based digital
systems also in their work.

00:19:29.920 --> 00:19:32.500
So I'm going to switch
over to the Chromecast now.

00:19:37.049 --> 00:19:38.340
Can we put it up on the screen?

00:19:43.310 --> 00:19:44.576
Can you see the Chromecast?

00:19:48.390 --> 00:19:49.140
Is it casting?

00:19:51.930 --> 00:19:53.652
I'll try and make sure--

00:19:58.162 --> 00:19:59.370
must have canceled out there.

00:19:59.370 --> 00:20:01.130
There we go.

00:20:01.130 --> 00:20:03.850
So as you can see when
we worked with them,

00:20:03.850 --> 00:20:06.760
we didn't want to give them
a typical kind of Android

00:20:06.760 --> 00:20:09.310
app that may be part of the
material design features

00:20:09.310 --> 00:20:10.690
that they might be used to.

00:20:10.690 --> 00:20:12.880
We wanted to give them something
that was very type of voice

00:20:12.880 --> 00:20:13.379
oriented.

00:20:13.379 --> 00:20:15.820
So you can see there's
no soft keys that we

00:20:15.820 --> 00:20:17.200
have here for navigation.

00:20:17.200 --> 00:20:19.390
They can completely
navigate by voice.

00:20:19.390 --> 00:20:20.920
Now, we did give
them an opportunity

00:20:20.920 --> 00:20:23.554
by using the sliding menu
to be also navigating.

00:20:23.554 --> 00:20:25.720
But we didn't give them any
buttons on the side here

00:20:25.720 --> 00:20:27.040
in order to navigate.

00:20:27.040 --> 00:20:29.570
And so they're easily
able to hear information.

00:20:29.570 --> 00:20:31.630
As you can see here,
this is an incident

00:20:31.630 --> 00:20:32.740
that they might be seeing.

00:20:32.740 --> 00:20:34.449
These are, of course,
made up incidences.

00:20:34.449 --> 00:20:36.573
But these are incidences
that they might be seeing.

00:20:36.573 --> 00:20:38.320
And so simply by
pushing on them,

00:20:38.320 --> 00:20:40.060
they can hear the information.

00:20:40.060 --> 00:20:42.652
Now when they get a report, they
may not just get one report,

00:20:42.652 --> 00:20:44.860
they may get additional
information as they're going.

00:20:44.860 --> 00:20:47.350
So you can see here there's all
different types of information

00:20:47.350 --> 00:20:48.070
that may be coming along.

00:20:48.070 --> 00:20:49.403
By simply just pushing on this--

00:20:51.970 --> 00:20:53.468
I don't think you can hear it.

00:21:00.660 --> 00:21:01.912
I click on the Do Not Disturb.

00:21:01.912 --> 00:21:03.120
That might be the reason why.

00:21:06.800 --> 00:21:09.377
Can you hear it?

00:21:09.377 --> 00:21:11.210
VIRTUAL ASSISTANT:
Gather witness statement.

00:21:11.210 --> 00:21:15.160
Location HA 0 1 at
Wembley London minus.

00:21:15.160 --> 00:21:18.690
Case number minus 128,781.

00:21:18.690 --> 00:21:20.800
Scout area for CCTV footage.

00:21:20.800 --> 00:21:23.694
Location SW 13-9 by
Hounslow London minus.

00:21:23.694 --> 00:21:26.110
PHILLIP REDMAN: So you can see
how the digital information

00:21:26.110 --> 00:21:28.266
keeps coming out, they
can hear all the updates.

00:21:28.266 --> 00:21:29.890
So that's one thing
that they could do.

00:21:29.890 --> 00:21:31.510
They could also navigate
the application.

00:21:31.510 --> 00:21:33.134
So if they wanted to
make a phone call,

00:21:33.134 --> 00:21:34.690
just like with a
regular assistant,

00:21:34.690 --> 00:21:38.110
they can push our
little button here.

00:21:38.110 --> 00:21:41.200
Go to blue k pages.

00:21:41.200 --> 00:21:44.050
VIRTUAL ASSISTANT:
Sorry, I didn't get that.

00:21:44.050 --> 00:21:46.380
PHILLIP REDMAN:
Go to blue pages.

00:21:46.380 --> 00:21:48.640
VIRTUAL ASSISTANT: OK,
showing the blue pages.

00:21:48.640 --> 00:21:50.380
PHILLIP REDMAN: So they can
either go to their directory

00:21:50.380 --> 00:21:51.140
that they have here.

00:21:51.140 --> 00:21:52.556
Or they can call
up someone's name

00:21:52.556 --> 00:21:55.042
and they can say,
call or message, etc.

00:21:55.042 --> 00:21:56.500
The other thing we
wanted to do was

00:21:56.500 --> 00:21:59.661
give them advanced capability
for taking witness statements.

00:21:59.661 --> 00:22:01.660
So today, they take all
their witness statements

00:22:01.660 --> 00:22:04.660
in the analog way, with
basically a pencil and paper.

00:22:04.660 --> 00:22:06.770
And so those may
not be accurate.

00:22:06.770 --> 00:22:10.530
Now, we can start a new
witness statement here.

00:22:10.530 --> 00:22:13.300
Start a new witness statement.

00:22:13.300 --> 00:22:17.330
VIRTUAL ASSISTANT: OK, here's
a new witness statement.

00:22:17.330 --> 00:22:19.520
PHILLIP REDMAN: And by
simply pushing the icon,

00:22:19.520 --> 00:22:22.250
they can start retrieving
information from the witness.

00:22:22.250 --> 00:22:23.960
And it takes the
notes right for them

00:22:23.960 --> 00:22:26.045
in the statement,
which they can go and--

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:31.230
oh, we shot off.

00:22:31.230 --> 00:22:31.960
There we go.

00:22:31.960 --> 00:22:33.168
Maybe a little nosy in here--

00:22:36.740 --> 00:22:39.157
they can take the notes right
on the witness statement

00:22:39.157 --> 00:22:40.490
and then go and edit them later.

00:22:43.466 --> 00:22:45.580
So you can see this comes up.

00:22:45.580 --> 00:22:48.505
We can also integrate this
into Google Translate.

00:22:48.505 --> 00:22:50.260
And so if there's
another language

00:22:50.260 --> 00:22:52.700
that someone's speaking,
they can then translate that

00:22:52.700 --> 00:22:54.220
onto English right there.

00:22:54.220 --> 00:22:57.250
Now today, they still need to
actually print out the witness

00:22:57.250 --> 00:22:58.660
statements and get them signed.

00:22:58.660 --> 00:23:00.340
So they can't use
this for court.

00:23:00.340 --> 00:23:02.860
But it gives them an accurate
way of collecting the data.

00:23:02.860 --> 00:23:05.690
Much more accurate than it
was with pencil and paper.

00:23:05.690 --> 00:23:08.560
So some pretty cool stuff using
a digital virtual assistant

00:23:08.560 --> 00:23:11.170
for the police force.

00:23:11.170 --> 00:23:14.140
All right, can we switch back
to the presentation now, please?

00:23:25.570 --> 00:23:29.477
So as you know, there
are some standard ways

00:23:29.477 --> 00:23:31.060
that we've been
developing mobile apps

00:23:31.060 --> 00:23:32.940
for many, many years.

00:23:32.940 --> 00:23:34.816
And we get asked this
quite a bit, what's

00:23:34.816 --> 00:23:36.190
the best way--
from our clients--

00:23:36.190 --> 00:23:39.630
what's the best way that we
should develop our mobile apps?

00:23:39.630 --> 00:23:42.070
What technology, what
platform should we be using?

00:23:42.070 --> 00:23:44.690
And a lot of it goes into,
who's the app being used for?

00:23:44.690 --> 00:23:45.880
Is it for the customer?

00:23:45.880 --> 00:23:47.290
Is it for their employees?

00:23:47.290 --> 00:23:48.530
Is it with a partner?

00:23:48.530 --> 00:23:49.780
What platforms are they using?

00:23:49.780 --> 00:23:52.330
Are they single or are
there multiple platforms?

00:23:52.330 --> 00:23:54.590
In many cases, the
majority of applications,

00:23:54.590 --> 00:23:56.950
especially for customers,
we develop still

00:23:56.950 --> 00:23:58.104
in the native format.

00:23:58.104 --> 00:23:59.770
And so most applications
today are still

00:23:59.770 --> 00:24:03.240
using the native capabilities,
whether the Android SDK

00:24:03.240 --> 00:24:05.290
or for Apple.

00:24:05.290 --> 00:24:07.510
It gives them the
richest type of interface

00:24:07.510 --> 00:24:09.695
that they can have,
high levels of security.

00:24:09.695 --> 00:24:12.070
It's something that everybody
wants to do today typically

00:24:12.070 --> 00:24:12.903
for their customers.

00:24:12.903 --> 00:24:14.860
And when you have a
large number of users

00:24:14.860 --> 00:24:17.640
that will be using that app,
it makes a lot of sense.

00:24:17.640 --> 00:24:20.200
But for internal users, it
may not make as much sense,

00:24:20.200 --> 00:24:22.420
because you have a
smaller internal employee

00:24:22.420 --> 00:24:23.687
force to develop that app.

00:24:23.687 --> 00:24:25.270
And there actually
may be, and what we

00:24:25.270 --> 00:24:28.840
see in most cases, multiple
platforms that are supported

00:24:28.840 --> 00:24:30.180
within the enterprise space.

00:24:30.180 --> 00:24:33.754
So they may support both
Android and Apple capabilities.

00:24:33.754 --> 00:24:35.170
And so they're
looking at building

00:24:35.170 --> 00:24:39.214
more of a hybrid
app that has kind

00:24:39.214 --> 00:24:41.130
of the best of both
worlds for native and web,

00:24:41.130 --> 00:24:44.380
a single development platform
to support the applications

00:24:44.380 --> 00:24:45.380
that they have.

00:24:45.380 --> 00:24:47.130
Obviously, there,
as an advantage

00:24:47.130 --> 00:24:49.330
is the operating pieces on that.

00:24:49.330 --> 00:24:52.019
The testing, the development,
and then the operating costs

00:24:52.019 --> 00:24:53.560
are much lower
because you don't have

00:24:53.560 --> 00:24:55.240
to support individual
applications

00:24:55.240 --> 00:24:56.290
and individual platforms.

00:24:56.290 --> 00:25:00.010
And so we're seeing an increased
demand from our customer base

00:25:00.010 --> 00:25:01.570
to support more of
these hybrid apps.

00:25:01.570 --> 00:25:03.986
And that's across a number of
different hybrid development

00:25:03.986 --> 00:25:05.500
technologies.

00:25:05.500 --> 00:25:08.235
Mobile web is an early
way for developing

00:25:08.235 --> 00:25:09.110
apps, web-based apps.

00:25:09.110 --> 00:25:11.407
But they haven't
been very responsive.

00:25:11.407 --> 00:25:13.240
And so obviously you
need a good connection.

00:25:13.240 --> 00:25:15.520
You don't have an
offline capability.

00:25:15.520 --> 00:25:17.150
They are OS agnostic.

00:25:17.150 --> 00:25:20.290
But the interface and
the usability of those

00:25:20.290 --> 00:25:21.410
typically are a lot lower.

00:25:21.410 --> 00:25:23.866
And so we don't really build
a lot of mobile web apps.

00:25:23.866 --> 00:25:25.240
They're just not
really demanded.

00:25:25.240 --> 00:25:26.830
But we think that there
are opportunities for this

00:25:26.830 --> 00:25:29.200
to change in the future with
kind of more of the next

00:25:29.200 --> 00:25:32.230
generation of mobile apps
that we're going to focus

00:25:32.230 --> 00:25:36.820
on, including-- how do I
get this thing to work--

00:25:36.820 --> 00:25:39.935
progressive web apps, micro
apps, and instant apps.

00:25:39.935 --> 00:25:42.310
Does anyone here have any
experience with progressive web

00:25:42.310 --> 00:25:42.809
apps at all?

00:25:42.809 --> 00:25:44.530
Raise your hand.

00:25:44.530 --> 00:25:45.970
Just one person.

00:25:45.970 --> 00:25:49.650
So been around for a little bit,
introduced not too long ago,

00:25:49.650 --> 00:25:52.150
but been around for a bit, where
you could take the benefits

00:25:52.150 --> 00:25:55.510
of native and increased
capability for mobile web

00:25:55.510 --> 00:25:58.090
apps-- so these are web apps--

00:25:58.090 --> 00:25:59.270
they're instantly shareable.

00:25:59.270 --> 00:25:59.980
There linkable.

00:25:59.980 --> 00:26:01.146
You can send someone a link.

00:26:01.146 --> 00:26:03.490
You don't need to download
these from an app store.

00:26:03.490 --> 00:26:05.560
And they have a
functionality that's

00:26:05.560 --> 00:26:09.630
improved performance
overall for mobile users.

00:26:09.630 --> 00:26:11.581
And so we see certainly
and playing around

00:26:11.581 --> 00:26:13.330
with some of these
different capabilities,

00:26:13.330 --> 00:26:17.320
only supported on Android
today, not supported on iOS.

00:26:17.320 --> 00:26:19.600
But we think that these could
be some interesting ways

00:26:19.600 --> 00:26:22.820
of improving some of that
experience for mobile web apps.

00:26:22.820 --> 00:26:25.540
And it gives a much lower
cost for development and lower

00:26:25.540 --> 00:26:27.891
operating costs overall.

00:26:27.891 --> 00:26:30.390
Micro apps are another way that
we think that are expanding.

00:26:30.390 --> 00:26:32.640
So micro apps are
highly targeted apps,

00:26:32.640 --> 00:26:36.339
very application specific
apps, very simple to use.

00:26:36.339 --> 00:26:38.130
They may live and sit
right on the desktop.

00:26:38.130 --> 00:26:40.387
They have a very limited
amount of functionality.

00:26:40.387 --> 00:26:42.720
An example of a micro app may
be something like weather,

00:26:42.720 --> 00:26:43.320
for instance.

00:26:43.320 --> 00:26:46.050
Or you could see in the future
where maybe Uber would put out

00:26:46.050 --> 00:26:49.020
a micro app, where all you
do is just push the button

00:26:49.020 --> 00:26:51.260
and in five minutes
your car shows up.

00:26:51.260 --> 00:26:53.760
So think about that, like if
you're familiar with the Amazon

00:26:53.760 --> 00:26:57.120
buttons where you just push it
and your case of water shows up

00:26:57.120 --> 00:26:58.780
a day or two later.

00:26:58.780 --> 00:27:00.934
The same thing for mobility,
just push the button

00:27:00.934 --> 00:27:02.100
and you get the information.

00:27:02.100 --> 00:27:05.617
So very, very low cost to
develop, very low cost to use.

00:27:05.617 --> 00:27:07.950
I mean, even today, we've
seen that apps and native apps

00:27:07.950 --> 00:27:10.800
have grown in complexity,
where they have so many windows

00:27:10.800 --> 00:27:13.560
and screens and clicks in
order to get the information.

00:27:13.560 --> 00:27:16.010
And mobility is all
about the here and now.

00:27:16.010 --> 00:27:18.061
Its about how to do that
much quicker and easier.

00:27:18.061 --> 00:27:19.560
And you can't get
quicker and easier

00:27:19.560 --> 00:27:21.930
than a micro app, which
can deliver the information

00:27:21.930 --> 00:27:23.745
or the service ASAP.

00:27:23.745 --> 00:27:26.370
And then finally, we're going to
show you and talk a little bit

00:27:26.370 --> 00:27:27.690
about these Instant Apps.

00:27:27.690 --> 00:27:29.360
Is anyone familiar
with Instant Apps

00:27:29.360 --> 00:27:31.480
introduced a few years ago?

00:27:31.480 --> 00:27:32.872
Not too many people here.

00:27:32.872 --> 00:27:34.330
So an Instant App
are app functions

00:27:34.330 --> 00:27:36.670
that launch without
downloading an app.

00:27:36.670 --> 00:27:38.590
So it's a web-based app.

00:27:38.590 --> 00:27:41.162
Now built in native
capabilities,

00:27:41.162 --> 00:27:43.120
but they can leverage
those native capabilities

00:27:43.120 --> 00:27:45.328
that make it much easier to
develop and much quicker.

00:27:45.328 --> 00:27:47.830
And I'll show you some examples
of what we mean by that.

00:27:47.830 --> 00:27:49.240
They are fairly limited.

00:27:49.240 --> 00:27:51.599
But they're very, very
easy to use and deploy.

00:27:51.599 --> 00:27:53.140
So they're great if
you're only using

00:27:53.140 --> 00:27:55.900
the app on a one-time basis
or for a short-term, temporary

00:27:55.900 --> 00:27:56.780
basis.

00:27:56.780 --> 00:27:59.020
So today even for things
like these conferences,

00:27:59.020 --> 00:28:02.360
we may download the conference
app and we use it for the week,

00:28:02.360 --> 00:28:04.572
but do we really need to
have it sit on our device?

00:28:04.572 --> 00:28:06.280
How many people here
have downloaded apps

00:28:06.280 --> 00:28:08.980
that they've used only
temporarily or for one-time use

00:28:08.980 --> 00:28:10.860
and then deleted it
off their device?

00:28:10.860 --> 00:28:13.400
With Instant Apps, you won't
have to do that anymore.

00:28:13.400 --> 00:28:16.310
Again, only available
for Android platforms.

00:28:16.310 --> 00:28:18.580
So I'm going to show you
kind of what that means.

00:28:18.580 --> 00:28:25.450
If we go back to the
Chromecast, this is a native app

00:28:25.450 --> 00:28:28.990
that we developed for a client
for a package delivery service

00:28:28.990 --> 00:28:30.040
last year.

00:28:30.040 --> 00:28:32.170
This is a full native app
that lives on the device.

00:28:32.170 --> 00:28:33.820
In a package
delivery service, you

00:28:33.820 --> 00:28:35.890
have people that are
scanning packages in,

00:28:35.890 --> 00:28:37.390
putting them on
trucks, and then you

00:28:37.390 --> 00:28:39.790
have people that are
delivering the packages.

00:28:39.790 --> 00:28:41.850
Now sometimes that's
the same person,

00:28:41.850 --> 00:28:43.640
but often times it's
a different person.

00:28:43.640 --> 00:28:46.570
So the driver who's driving
and delivering those packages

00:28:46.570 --> 00:28:49.090
would get this app, but they
might not do any scanning.

00:28:49.090 --> 00:28:52.645
So when you log into this
app, you can see here there's

00:28:52.645 --> 00:28:54.520
an ability that they
could just click on here

00:28:54.520 --> 00:28:56.500
and they can scan packages in.

00:28:56.500 --> 00:29:01.500
So if we have my little
bar code reader here,

00:29:01.500 --> 00:29:02.460
it reads the bar code.

00:29:02.460 --> 00:29:03.390
It scans the package.

00:29:03.390 --> 00:29:04.650
You get the information in.

00:29:04.650 --> 00:29:06.450
But this is a full
native app, including

00:29:06.450 --> 00:29:08.700
turn by turn directions
and instructions

00:29:08.700 --> 00:29:12.360
that we have built on
Google and Android platform,

00:29:12.360 --> 00:29:15.124
complete with Google
Firebase on the back end.

00:29:15.124 --> 00:29:17.040
On the front end, we
have Google Maps in here.

00:29:17.040 --> 00:29:18.956
We have image recognition,
all different types

00:29:18.956 --> 00:29:20.940
of capabilities for this.

00:29:20.940 --> 00:29:22.770
But for a person
just scanning it,

00:29:22.770 --> 00:29:25.460
do they really need to have
this full type of application?

00:29:25.460 --> 00:29:26.710
So I have another device here.

00:29:31.129 --> 00:29:32.420
I'm going to bring this one up.

00:29:36.760 --> 00:29:38.850
And this is going to
show you an Instant App

00:29:38.850 --> 00:29:41.400
that we developed that kind
of does the same thing.

00:29:49.170 --> 00:29:53.370
And so let's say the package
scanner comes into work,

00:29:53.370 --> 00:29:54.510
we can send them an email.

00:29:54.510 --> 00:29:56.093
So just through this
email right here,

00:29:56.093 --> 00:29:57.930
you can see they get a link.

00:29:57.930 --> 00:30:02.340
No downloading anything from app
store, no pushing anything out,

00:30:02.340 --> 00:30:06.790
simply click on the link, and an
Instant App will open right up.

00:30:06.790 --> 00:30:09.940
They could just log in with
their credentials and then,

00:30:09.940 --> 00:30:16.090
boom, start scanning in
seconds, the same type of idea.

00:30:16.090 --> 00:30:19.084
Very, very low cost to
develop, very easy to do.

00:30:19.084 --> 00:30:20.500
And then, boom,
when they're done,

00:30:20.500 --> 00:30:23.140
they just click out of the
app and then they're done.

00:30:23.140 --> 00:30:25.690
They can save the link for
the future, whatever that is.

00:30:25.690 --> 00:30:27.100
And so we can send them
a link every morning.

00:30:27.100 --> 00:30:28.640
They can put it
in their calendar.

00:30:28.640 --> 00:30:30.260
So very easy to use.

00:30:30.260 --> 00:30:32.560
So you can imagine a plethora
of different ideas of how

00:30:32.560 --> 00:30:34.060
you could use these
Android Instant

00:30:34.060 --> 00:30:36.790
Apps for both
potential temporary use

00:30:36.790 --> 00:30:38.900
or for short-term use
or for people that don't

00:30:38.900 --> 00:30:40.750
need a full app capabilities.

00:30:40.750 --> 00:30:43.930
So not a lot of this being
used in the marketplace yet,

00:30:43.930 --> 00:30:45.410
but we see a whole
lot of interest.

00:30:45.410 --> 00:30:47.493
And we think there are a
whole lot of capabilities

00:30:47.493 --> 00:30:48.660
to come out with this.

00:30:48.660 --> 00:30:51.076
All right, we can go back to
the presentation now, please.

00:30:54.610 --> 00:30:56.980
So those are two examples
of us working with clients

00:30:56.980 --> 00:30:59.188
and developing and using
some of these new generation

00:30:59.188 --> 00:30:59.960
technologies.

00:30:59.960 --> 00:31:03.520
The first one using capabilities
like machine learning and AI.

00:31:03.520 --> 00:31:05.374
So with the natural
voice recognition,

00:31:05.374 --> 00:31:06.790
you can see how
easily that works,

00:31:06.790 --> 00:31:08.950
even within the
conference hall here

00:31:08.950 --> 00:31:12.432
when it's noisy, and
natural language speech,

00:31:12.432 --> 00:31:14.140
all those types of
things that we can use

00:31:14.140 --> 00:31:17.290
using the Google technologies.

00:31:17.290 --> 00:31:20.860
So capabilities, they're
simplifying in many ways.

00:31:20.860 --> 00:31:22.760
But they're offering
more capabilities.

00:31:22.760 --> 00:31:25.870
So with these micro
apps, some examples here,

00:31:25.870 --> 00:31:28.150
simply maybe ordering
something off a menu,

00:31:28.150 --> 00:31:30.130
approving a vacation
request, approving

00:31:30.130 --> 00:31:35.320
T&amp;E. Different microflow, so
integration now into enterprise

00:31:35.320 --> 00:31:38.960
application platforms like
Salesforce.com or Workday

00:31:38.960 --> 00:31:41.530
and ServiceNow, so
capabilities of supporting that

00:31:41.530 --> 00:31:44.260
not just a web-based
capability, but integrated

00:31:44.260 --> 00:31:46.420
into really robust information.

00:31:46.420 --> 00:31:48.220
And then microservices--
so as we all know,

00:31:48.220 --> 00:31:50.620
microservices are
growing in demand

00:31:50.620 --> 00:31:52.584
to incorporate more
support and capabilities

00:31:52.584 --> 00:31:54.250
across these different
types of devices.

00:31:57.290 --> 00:31:59.680
So what does this take
in order to support

00:31:59.680 --> 00:32:01.592
a lot of these next
gen app capabilities?

00:32:01.592 --> 00:32:03.550
You've heard about some
stuff on the front end.

00:32:03.550 --> 00:32:07.210
So we have hardware,
software, network capabilities

00:32:07.210 --> 00:32:10.390
that have evolved to support
a much richer capability

00:32:10.390 --> 00:32:11.770
for these mobile apps.

00:32:11.770 --> 00:32:14.140
So the bottom line is
we feel with our clients

00:32:14.140 --> 00:32:15.720
there's no excuses anymore.

00:32:15.720 --> 00:32:17.290
There's nothing holding us back.

00:32:17.290 --> 00:32:19.720
Even costs have come
down-- costs of hardware,

00:32:19.720 --> 00:32:22.270
for services, for
cloud capabilities.

00:32:22.270 --> 00:32:24.970
There's really very few barriers
to do almost anything out there

00:32:24.970 --> 00:32:28.270
today, except for there
is an issue of back end.

00:32:28.270 --> 00:32:31.144
What we run into all the
time is back end complexity.

00:32:31.144 --> 00:32:32.560
So depending on
the industry, they

00:32:32.560 --> 00:32:33.730
could be a regulated industry.

00:32:33.730 --> 00:32:36.271
They could be an industry that's
been around for a long time.

00:32:36.271 --> 00:32:37.990
They don't have APIs
that are developed,

00:32:37.990 --> 00:32:40.750
and they don't have an
easy way of accessing

00:32:40.750 --> 00:32:41.817
their corporate data.

00:32:41.817 --> 00:32:43.900
If we could just erase
everything from our clients

00:32:43.900 --> 00:32:46.330
and start over, the world
would be much simpler,

00:32:46.330 --> 00:32:48.660
but it be a very, very
expensive place to go.

00:32:48.660 --> 00:32:51.256
And so how do we access
that data for them

00:32:51.256 --> 00:32:53.380
to put it onto these kind
of front end capabilities

00:32:53.380 --> 00:32:54.520
that we're talking about?

00:32:54.520 --> 00:32:57.234
So we're not only thinking about
that front end development,

00:32:57.234 --> 00:32:58.150
but also the back end.

00:32:58.150 --> 00:32:59.691
And there's lots of
cool stuff that's

00:32:59.691 --> 00:33:02.140
happening on the back end
to make this all work.

00:33:02.140 --> 00:33:05.320
So we're going from
monolithic structures, which

00:33:05.320 --> 00:33:08.440
have been easy to deploy,
no scalability issues,

00:33:08.440 --> 00:33:11.500
but difficult to add features,
to much more scalable,

00:33:11.500 --> 00:33:13.960
with client server
capabilities, and now

00:33:13.960 --> 00:33:15.730
into more micro services.

00:33:15.730 --> 00:33:19.060
And so a micro service is a
platform and an architecture,

00:33:19.060 --> 00:33:21.820
software architecture,
that can easily integrate

00:33:21.820 --> 00:33:22.982
into business services.

00:33:22.982 --> 00:33:24.940
And it can break down
the different application

00:33:24.940 --> 00:33:27.310
needs into these
different services

00:33:27.310 --> 00:33:29.590
that you can easily
build once and then

00:33:29.590 --> 00:33:32.510
use across all different
types of mobile platforms.

00:33:32.510 --> 00:33:34.730
So you can eliminate a
single point of failure,

00:33:34.730 --> 00:33:37.042
because if one, let's say,
business service goes down,

00:33:37.042 --> 00:33:39.250
there's still other services
that would be available.

00:33:39.250 --> 00:33:41.520
There isn't one
level of integration.

00:33:41.520 --> 00:33:42.940
They're very, very scalable.

00:33:42.940 --> 00:33:45.190
And most importantly,
they're reusable.

00:33:45.190 --> 00:33:47.202
So we could build
stuff for some clients.

00:33:47.202 --> 00:33:48.910
And then we can reuse
those microservices

00:33:48.910 --> 00:33:50.170
across different clients.

00:33:50.170 --> 00:33:52.330
Very easy, very
fast, very, very low

00:33:52.330 --> 00:33:54.070
cost to build these services.

00:33:54.070 --> 00:33:55.630
They're very, very
rich too, so they

00:33:55.630 --> 00:33:57.880
can support complex
orchestration

00:33:57.880 --> 00:34:00.940
across different data stores,
across different platforms,

00:34:00.940 --> 00:34:03.010
across different types
of mobile devices,

00:34:03.010 --> 00:34:04.910
across different data types.

00:34:04.910 --> 00:34:07.030
So very, very,
very complex stuff

00:34:07.030 --> 00:34:09.340
that's built down and
integrated very easily.

00:34:09.340 --> 00:34:11.739
And they use this business
logic layer as a service.

00:34:11.739 --> 00:34:13.408
It's a new digital
orchestration layer

00:34:13.408 --> 00:34:14.949
that we think that's
really important

00:34:14.949 --> 00:34:17.830
that's evolved in part of the
mobile back end as a service

00:34:17.830 --> 00:34:19.989
capability.

00:34:19.989 --> 00:34:23.139
So going from monolithic to
more modular capabilities.

00:34:23.139 --> 00:34:25.989
So we can take these different
services and drop them in place

00:34:25.989 --> 00:34:28.330
and customize those for
different types of employees,

00:34:28.330 --> 00:34:29.889
for different
types of customers,

00:34:29.889 --> 00:34:31.960
for different types
of needs on the go,

00:34:31.960 --> 00:34:34.139
again, using the front end
capabilities of Instant

00:34:34.139 --> 00:34:37.447
Apps, progressive web
apps, micro apps, etc.

00:34:37.447 --> 00:34:39.530
They flow across all those
different capabilities.

00:34:39.530 --> 00:34:42.489
So we can customize whatever
those employee needs are,

00:34:42.489 --> 00:34:46.060
whatever the mobile needs are,
to the back end capabilities,

00:34:46.060 --> 00:34:49.236
instead of having to
build that out each time.

00:34:49.236 --> 00:34:50.860
That's what we really
have to do today.

00:34:50.860 --> 00:34:52.960
We typically have to build
something specialized

00:34:52.960 --> 00:34:54.550
out each time.

00:34:54.550 --> 00:34:57.230
And that's very, very
time consuming and very,

00:34:57.230 --> 00:34:58.320
very expensive.

00:34:58.320 --> 00:35:01.450
And so where we're going
from 12 to 24 months

00:35:01.450 --> 00:35:03.040
to build these
things out, we can

00:35:03.040 --> 00:35:05.910
do these in a matter
of days in many cases.

00:35:05.910 --> 00:35:09.880
So very, very rich, and very,
very awesome capabilities.

00:35:09.880 --> 00:35:11.980
It also doesn't exclude
any of the intelligence

00:35:11.980 --> 00:35:14.200
that these next
generation apps need.

00:35:14.200 --> 00:35:16.360
So whether the apps
need to be deployed

00:35:16.360 --> 00:35:17.950
or they don't have
context, they can't

00:35:17.950 --> 00:35:19.840
see the backend
capabilities or they're

00:35:19.840 --> 00:35:21.900
very limited in
what they can see,

00:35:21.900 --> 00:35:25.000
right now we can add in
that backend intelligence.

00:35:25.000 --> 00:35:27.610
Same intelligence that we
see on more richer apps,

00:35:27.610 --> 00:35:30.930
we could even do that
on these basic apps too.

00:35:30.930 --> 00:35:33.950
We can support connected
capabilities on App Services.

00:35:33.950 --> 00:35:36.650
So there's a common
framework that integrates

00:35:36.650 --> 00:35:37.970
these disconnected back ends.

00:35:37.970 --> 00:35:40.920
And we can support them
offline, real time configuration

00:35:40.920 --> 00:35:44.060
of new events or engagements
or anything that comes out.

00:35:44.060 --> 00:35:47.239
All doing that on
the flow, on the go.

00:35:47.239 --> 00:35:49.280
And so as you may be
familiar with, a lot of this

00:35:49.280 --> 00:35:51.770
is done through a mobile
as a backend system.

00:35:51.770 --> 00:35:53.660
Is anyone here
familiar with MBES,

00:35:53.660 --> 00:35:56.980
mobile as a backend system,
if you raise your hand.

00:35:56.980 --> 00:35:58.770
Oh, OK, not too many people.

00:35:58.770 --> 00:36:01.590
Surprisingly, these things have
been out for a long, long time,

00:36:01.590 --> 00:36:03.240
over six, seven years.

00:36:03.240 --> 00:36:06.210
And the idea was that this
mobile as a backend system

00:36:06.210 --> 00:36:09.640
would be a platform
that does two things.

00:36:09.640 --> 00:36:12.134
One would be a very quick
and easy development

00:36:12.134 --> 00:36:14.550
that works across all different
types of mobile platforms.

00:36:14.550 --> 00:36:17.250
So you can develop a
mobile front end, typically

00:36:17.250 --> 00:36:21.161
in a hybrid app capability to
support any type of platform,

00:36:21.161 --> 00:36:23.160
whether it's Android,
whether it was BlackBerry,

00:36:23.160 --> 00:36:25.780
whether it was Apple.

00:36:25.780 --> 00:36:27.240
So easy way to develop.

00:36:27.240 --> 00:36:30.240
The second part of it was,
though, an easy integration.

00:36:30.240 --> 00:36:33.220
So APIs built right
into the application,

00:36:33.220 --> 00:36:36.570
a mobile backend piece that was
integrated into your business

00:36:36.570 --> 00:36:39.240
services to be able
to support and get

00:36:39.240 --> 00:36:42.180
the data from your back end
onto these mobile devices very

00:36:42.180 --> 00:36:43.919
easy and very cheaply.

00:36:43.919 --> 00:36:45.960
So like I said, this has
been around for a while.

00:36:45.960 --> 00:36:48.330
As you can see, nobody
here has been using them.

00:36:48.330 --> 00:36:50.130
And we don't see a
whole lot of use in this

00:36:50.130 --> 00:36:52.764
yet, because there have
been some challenges

00:36:52.764 --> 00:36:53.430
on the back end.

00:36:53.430 --> 00:36:55.579
Basically the front end
hasn't been very rich.

00:36:55.579 --> 00:36:57.120
There was still more
development that

00:36:57.120 --> 00:36:59.036
needed to be done on
those front ends in order

00:36:59.036 --> 00:36:59.790
to make them work.

00:36:59.790 --> 00:37:01.248
And so again, most
people have been

00:37:01.248 --> 00:37:03.120
kind of focusing on
consumer applications

00:37:03.120 --> 00:37:05.190
and going straight in to
that kind of front end

00:37:05.190 --> 00:37:07.690
development and native
application development.

00:37:07.690 --> 00:37:11.250
So what we see here is with the
emergence of the microservices

00:37:11.250 --> 00:37:14.190
we can add in a new layer to
increase the capabilities, what

00:37:14.190 --> 00:37:16.650
we're calling this digital
orchestration layer,

00:37:16.650 --> 00:37:19.020
to an existing mobile
backend service

00:37:19.020 --> 00:37:21.420
to start supporting
those microservices

00:37:21.420 --> 00:37:22.840
that I mentioned before.

00:37:22.840 --> 00:37:25.505
So then a very, very quick
way and very low cost way,

00:37:25.505 --> 00:37:27.380
whether it's a native
app, whether it's a web

00:37:27.380 --> 00:37:30.700
app, a hybrid app, to support
the backend capability.

00:37:30.700 --> 00:37:33.240
So it's going to bring in a
lot more intelligence and a lot

00:37:33.240 --> 00:37:35.580
easier to develop and such.

00:37:35.580 --> 00:37:38.250
And so obviously, this does
increase the number of APIs

00:37:38.250 --> 00:37:40.500
that the organization
may need to build out

00:37:40.500 --> 00:37:42.840
in order to support
those different services.

00:37:42.840 --> 00:37:45.470
But we believe that this will
be a much faster and easier way

00:37:45.470 --> 00:37:49.350
and maybe more successful way of
getting these applications out

00:37:49.350 --> 00:37:50.920
into the hands,
getting the data out

00:37:50.920 --> 00:37:52.680
into the hands of
the mobile workers.

00:37:52.680 --> 00:37:55.950
As Nisha said, we have all these
technologies and capabilities

00:37:55.950 --> 00:37:58.264
and the consumer world has
really grown dramatically,

00:37:58.264 --> 00:37:59.680
but the enterprise
world has still

00:37:59.680 --> 00:38:03.350
have been very limited in
support for a lot of the data.

00:38:03.350 --> 00:38:05.470
And so for these next
generation app services

00:38:05.470 --> 00:38:08.230
we want to look at, things
to think about in the future

00:38:08.230 --> 00:38:10.970
are the support for
more liquid apps.

00:38:10.970 --> 00:38:12.460
So liquid has
become a term that's

00:38:12.460 --> 00:38:14.350
fairly popular out there.

00:38:14.350 --> 00:38:17.630
But what we mean with this
is much more agile apps.

00:38:17.630 --> 00:38:19.990
So apps that can be
changed on the fly, where

00:38:19.990 --> 00:38:21.520
new data can be
brought in, where

00:38:21.520 --> 00:38:23.020
they can be updated on the fly.

00:38:23.020 --> 00:38:25.570
So we don't want to think of
these apps as just static.

00:38:25.570 --> 00:38:28.090
And every time there's
an update, to the OS,

00:38:28.090 --> 00:38:29.632
we need to change
some of those apps.

00:38:29.632 --> 00:38:31.131
So what's a better
way of doing that

00:38:31.131 --> 00:38:32.770
versus kind of
rewriting the whole app

00:38:32.770 --> 00:38:34.930
or having to patch
those apps all the time?

00:38:34.930 --> 00:38:36.679
So we have to think
of new ways to support

00:38:36.679 --> 00:38:37.780
these kind of liquid apps.

00:38:37.780 --> 00:38:40.270
Connected App Services are
another huge consideration

00:38:40.270 --> 00:38:42.196
when it comes down to
the app capabilities.

00:38:42.196 --> 00:38:44.070
So we want to look at
the different services,

00:38:44.070 --> 00:38:45.920
so with the backend
capabilities,

00:38:45.920 --> 00:38:49.960
whether they're intelligent
services, Salesforce.com

00:38:49.960 --> 00:38:52.844
capabilities, or ERP, whatever
that might be out there.

00:38:52.844 --> 00:38:54.760
So how can we support
these different services

00:38:54.760 --> 00:38:56.380
and add context to that?

00:38:56.380 --> 00:38:58.870
So it knows my location, just
like we showed on the map

00:38:58.870 --> 00:39:01.180
with Enterprise 3.0,
it knows where we're

00:39:01.180 --> 00:39:04.390
standing on the map, so
it can recommend coffee

00:39:04.390 --> 00:39:07.600
for us or other things that
we might want during our day.

00:39:07.600 --> 00:39:10.040
Can we do the same thing
for the enterprise?

00:39:10.040 --> 00:39:12.670
So an example on that
is one of our clients

00:39:12.670 --> 00:39:15.400
wanted to be able to build
in intelligence services

00:39:15.400 --> 00:39:18.100
to their Salesforce.com
that showed them on the map

00:39:18.100 --> 00:39:20.560
where their customers are and
then be able to kind of look

00:39:20.560 --> 00:39:23.650
into those customer base and
see what are their recent sales

00:39:23.650 --> 00:39:26.620
or even use machine learning
to make recommendations

00:39:26.620 --> 00:39:29.480
of new opportunities to
sell within that customer.

00:39:29.480 --> 00:39:31.480
So of course, most
salespeople have a schedule

00:39:31.480 --> 00:39:32.560
and they have a
calendar that they're

00:39:32.560 --> 00:39:33.950
following during the day.

00:39:33.950 --> 00:39:36.040
But we also know that
meetings get canceled

00:39:36.040 --> 00:39:38.080
or they may find some
more time during the day.

00:39:38.080 --> 00:39:39.955
So what's the easiest
way to kind of find out

00:39:39.955 --> 00:39:42.490
how to optimize that downtime?

00:39:42.490 --> 00:39:45.650
So this whole idea of
assisted selling is huge.

00:39:45.650 --> 00:39:47.830
Now by simply
looking onto the map,

00:39:47.830 --> 00:39:49.270
by seeing where
the customers are,

00:39:49.270 --> 00:39:51.210
finding customer
information, they're

00:39:51.210 --> 00:39:52.960
able to then make
recommendations and then

00:39:52.960 --> 00:39:54.987
potentially do
additional revenue

00:39:54.987 --> 00:39:57.070
that they hadn't thought
of during the day before.

00:39:57.070 --> 00:39:59.200
So lots of intelligent
app services that we see

00:39:59.200 --> 00:40:01.060
are being built into these apps.

00:40:01.060 --> 00:40:04.210
Now, of course, with those
capabilities, lots of API calls

00:40:04.210 --> 00:40:05.900
are going to need to be made.

00:40:05.900 --> 00:40:07.630
So they need to have
high performance app

00:40:07.630 --> 00:40:09.589
services that can scale.

00:40:09.589 --> 00:40:11.380
So we test out for
those apps all the time,

00:40:11.380 --> 00:40:13.810
but that's going to be a huge
consideration, because there's

00:40:13.810 --> 00:40:14.980
going to be more and
more data coming.

00:40:14.980 --> 00:40:17.380
Whether it's from the cloud,
whether it's from on-premise

00:40:17.380 --> 00:40:20.200
on the enterprise, wherever
that may be, lots of API

00:40:20.200 --> 00:40:22.700
calls that are going to
be made during the day.

00:40:22.700 --> 00:40:24.510
It needs to be digital ready.

00:40:24.510 --> 00:40:26.750
And so that means how
can we use analytics

00:40:26.750 --> 00:40:30.350
in order to improve whatever
the application may be?

00:40:30.350 --> 00:40:31.850
How can we improve
the interface?

00:40:31.850 --> 00:40:34.620
How can we improve
the mobile experience?

00:40:34.620 --> 00:40:37.910
How can we make sure that
security, although is strong,

00:40:37.910 --> 00:40:40.500
doesn't become a barrier to
use of these different apps?

00:40:40.500 --> 00:40:42.814
And so digital ready is
a huge piece on that.

00:40:42.814 --> 00:40:45.230
And then as I mentioned with
the new digital orchestration

00:40:45.230 --> 00:40:47.630
services, how can
we make sure that we

00:40:47.630 --> 00:40:49.220
have the right APIs
that are easily

00:40:49.220 --> 00:40:51.810
built into those apps for
today and in the future

00:40:51.810 --> 00:40:55.100
to improve those services and
add services on to make them

00:40:55.100 --> 00:40:57.680
ready to go in the future?

00:40:57.680 --> 00:40:59.500
So some key
recommendations and then we

00:40:59.500 --> 00:41:02.560
can take some questions.

00:41:02.560 --> 00:41:05.940
So we think around these
next generation apps,

00:41:05.940 --> 00:41:08.170
they're not just aligning
to your digital strategy

00:41:08.170 --> 00:41:10.690
and the transformation
at most organizations,

00:41:10.690 --> 00:41:14.920
but they're going to
become a big part, if not

00:41:14.920 --> 00:41:18.517
the part of their digital
transformation strategy.

00:41:18.517 --> 00:41:20.350
A lot of the data and
the traffic as you see

00:41:20.350 --> 00:41:22.120
has already moved
off of desktops

00:41:22.120 --> 00:41:23.980
and moving onto mobile devices.

00:41:23.980 --> 00:41:26.110
And so this has to
fully align and really

00:41:26.110 --> 00:41:29.020
help drive a lot of the
transformation strategy.

00:41:29.020 --> 00:41:31.660
Also put a strategy together
to help modernize your app

00:41:31.660 --> 00:41:32.592
architectures.

00:41:32.592 --> 00:41:34.300
And so the best thing
that we see on this

00:41:34.300 --> 00:41:38.260
is that it's no longer siloed
between your mobile device,

00:41:38.260 --> 00:41:40.390
between your portable
devices, and then, of course,

00:41:40.390 --> 00:41:42.610
between your laptop or
your desktop devices

00:41:42.610 --> 00:41:43.900
that may be being used.

00:41:43.900 --> 00:41:45.970
A lot of these capabilities
can go across any

00:41:45.970 --> 00:41:47.380
of those different platforms.

00:41:47.380 --> 00:41:50.860
And so it's a write once work
anywhere type of scenario,

00:41:50.860 --> 00:41:52.930
which builds a big
integrated approach

00:41:52.930 --> 00:41:54.670
and helps reduce the
time for development

00:41:54.670 --> 00:41:56.600
and reduce the
cost in the future.

00:41:56.600 --> 00:41:59.020
And so when we're doing the
strategy for our clients,

00:41:59.020 --> 00:42:01.990
we're not looking at mobility
as a standalone capability.

00:42:01.990 --> 00:42:04.220
We're looking at as
part of the solution

00:42:04.220 --> 00:42:07.070
on how to take data from
one area to the next,

00:42:07.070 --> 00:42:09.760
from one device to
the next, and how

00:42:09.760 --> 00:42:11.890
to think about the
data access and not

00:42:11.890 --> 00:42:13.790
think about the
device or the network.

00:42:13.790 --> 00:42:15.992
We don't have to worry
about that as much anymore.

00:42:15.992 --> 00:42:17.950
And then there's always
going to be the support

00:42:17.950 --> 00:42:19.582
for multiple ecosystems.

00:42:19.582 --> 00:42:21.790
So there are going to be
new ecosystems in the future

00:42:21.790 --> 00:42:23.380
that maybe we're
not thinking about.

00:42:23.380 --> 00:42:25.213
But we want to make
sure that it's something

00:42:25.213 --> 00:42:27.340
that we can easily apply
and move in the future,

00:42:27.340 --> 00:42:30.610
where today we're building out
and customizing and integrating

00:42:30.610 --> 00:42:33.191
a lot of capabilities because
of these monolithic systems

00:42:33.191 --> 00:42:35.690
that we've had in the past, of
course, which weren't digital

00:42:35.690 --> 00:42:37.840
ready, which weren't
designed for mobility.

00:42:37.840 --> 00:42:39.621
But think about in
the future, there

00:42:39.621 --> 00:42:41.120
could be different
types of devices.

00:42:41.120 --> 00:42:44.720
So we showed devices around
wearables, for instance.

00:42:44.720 --> 00:42:47.170
So we see more and more
interest in wearables for sure,

00:42:47.170 --> 00:42:49.336
whether that's something
that you wear on your wrist

00:42:49.336 --> 00:42:52.450
or nurses can wear
wearables around their neck.

00:42:52.450 --> 00:42:54.400
Or we're working
with a cruise line

00:42:54.400 --> 00:42:58.000
that wants to put wearables and
get rid of keys and car keys.

00:42:58.000 --> 00:43:01.340
And so it becomes more and more
part of what people are using.

00:43:01.340 --> 00:43:03.732
So it could be different
devices, different hardware

00:43:03.732 --> 00:43:04.940
pieces that people are using.

00:43:04.940 --> 00:43:06.490
So different types
of ecosystems.

00:43:06.490 --> 00:43:10.160
And building customized
apps doesn't go away.

00:43:10.160 --> 00:43:12.370
So as we mentioned, there's
a small percentage today

00:43:12.370 --> 00:43:13.780
of off-the-shelf
app development.

00:43:13.780 --> 00:43:16.230
It's only around
30% of apps today

00:43:16.230 --> 00:43:17.990
are purely just off-the-shelf.

00:43:17.990 --> 00:43:19.630
So the majority,
the vast majority

00:43:19.630 --> 00:43:21.760
takes some customization.

00:43:21.760 --> 00:43:24.250
The idea of the templatized
apps that Nisha talked about

00:43:24.250 --> 00:43:26.920
was that there would
be 80% of the work done

00:43:26.920 --> 00:43:29.309
with 20% customization,
it turns out

00:43:29.309 --> 00:43:31.100
it's a lot more than
that needs to be done.

00:43:31.100 --> 00:43:33.550
So there's always customization
that needs to be done.

00:43:33.550 --> 00:43:34.960
So we're not ever going
to get away from that,

00:43:34.960 --> 00:43:37.290
but you can see there's
different ways to customize.

00:43:37.290 --> 00:43:39.560
And we don't look at everything
the same way anymore.

00:43:39.560 --> 00:43:41.470
So now when we walk
into our clients,

00:43:41.470 --> 00:43:43.930
we can figure out what
the requirements are first

00:43:43.930 --> 00:43:46.480
and then decide, OK,
is it a native app?

00:43:46.480 --> 00:43:47.580
Is it a web-based app?

00:43:47.580 --> 00:43:48.580
Will it be a hybrid app?

00:43:48.580 --> 00:43:51.390
Or some of these micro
apps and Instant Apps

00:43:51.390 --> 00:43:53.202
are also options
that we can use.

00:43:53.202 --> 00:43:54.910
And so we're not just
building a one size

00:43:54.910 --> 00:43:55.952
fits all for our clients.

00:43:55.952 --> 00:43:57.326
We have different
ways, depending

00:43:57.326 --> 00:43:58.910
on the requirements
of their speed,

00:43:58.910 --> 00:44:00.910
what their budget might
be, what the application

00:44:00.910 --> 00:44:03.616
requirements are, their users,
the devices that they're using.

00:44:03.616 --> 00:44:05.490
And so we're building
a whole different types

00:44:05.490 --> 00:44:06.862
of different systems for them.

00:44:06.862 --> 00:44:08.320
And we're doing
that intelligently.

00:44:08.320 --> 00:44:10.790
Like I mentioned, almost
everything we're doing today,

00:44:10.790 --> 00:44:13.570
especially out of our team
with digital transformation,

00:44:13.570 --> 00:44:16.540
includes machine
learning, whether it's

00:44:16.540 --> 00:44:18.550
voice-based capabilities
or moving away

00:44:18.550 --> 00:44:21.537
from navigating the app
through the finger and all

00:44:21.537 --> 00:44:22.120
through voice.

00:44:22.120 --> 00:44:22.990
And we think that's
something that's

00:44:22.990 --> 00:44:24.156
going to grow in the future.

00:44:24.156 --> 00:44:25.870
And we've seen
great responsiveness

00:44:25.870 --> 00:44:28.690
and great capabilities across
these devices for using this.

00:44:28.690 --> 00:44:29.710
So we think something
like that is

00:44:29.710 --> 00:44:31.450
going to become more of the
future of these different.

00:44:31.450 --> 00:44:33.370
So we're thinking
about navigation.

00:44:33.370 --> 00:44:35.170
We think about the
development, you

00:44:35.170 --> 00:44:37.753
know, lots of changes that are
happening in these applications

00:44:37.753 --> 00:44:39.790
today and that
will be continuing.

00:44:39.790 --> 00:44:42.970
We have a mobile point of
view, or a point of view

00:44:42.970 --> 00:44:45.494
in a white paper, that goes
into a lot more detail on this.

00:44:45.494 --> 00:44:47.410
And if you want to give
me your business card,

00:44:47.410 --> 00:44:50.052
I'm happy to send
you the link to that.

00:44:50.052 --> 00:44:51.760
And you can get more
detailed information

00:44:51.760 --> 00:44:53.979
both on the front end and
the back end development

00:44:53.979 --> 00:44:55.270
to kind of walk away with this.

00:44:55.270 --> 00:44:57.353
And then, of course, the
slides will be available.

00:44:57.353 --> 00:44:59.810
And this will be available
on YouTube later today.

00:44:59.810 --> 00:45:02.240
So thank you very much
for your attention.

00:45:02.240 --> 00:45:04.900
We have a few minutes left
to take some questions.

00:45:04.900 --> 00:45:06.300
Nisha will come back up.

00:45:06.300 --> 00:45:07.300
Any questions out there?

00:45:07.300 --> 00:45:09.650
If you do, please come up
and speak in the microphone,

00:45:09.650 --> 00:45:12.997
because we will be recording
all this for posterity later.

00:45:12.997 --> 00:45:14.830
NISHA SHARMA: Yeah, I
